  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 120 PM MDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nCurrent Water Vapor and upper air models show northwest flow over\nthe region, as broad upper ridge continues to sit over the west\ncoast, and upper trough over the Great Lakes and New England\nregion. 19Z surface analysis shows low pressure over the central ND/SD\nborder. Winds are generally light and variable across the \nforecast area, with current afternoon temperatures in the mid 70s \nto low 80s. \n\nVery little change to the overall pattern through the rest of the\nweek. The surface low passing through today will cause a slight\nincrease in winds this evening into tonight due to pressure rises\nbehind it, however winds still remain relatively light. Otherwise\nexpect dry and mild conditions to continue through Saturday. Low\nlevel southwesterly flow will bring in dry and warmer air for\nFriday and Saturday, with high temperatures climbing into the 80s\nand even low 90s for some areas. Good mixing will also result in\nstronger winds, with gusts 30 to 40 mph possible over the plains\nareas for both afternoons. The dry air coupled with stronger winds\nwill result in near-critical to critical fire weather conditions.\nA Fire Weather Watch has been issued for Friday late morning into\nthe evening for the northeast WY plains, southwestern SD, and the\nfoothills. \n\nThe pattern will change the latter half of the weekend, as upper\ntrough digs into the PAC NW and the Rockies. Temperatures will\nswing in the other direction, with highs dropping 25 to 30 degrees\nfor Sunday. Early next week will see our first chance for\nprecipitation as well, as a disturbance moves through in the\nSunday/Monday timeframe. With the cooler temperatures moving in,\nsome areas could see their first snow as well, depending on timing\nof the precipitation. QPF amounts are generally low with this wave\nthough, and given temperatures dont typically dip below freezing,\naccumulating snow is not expected. \n\nModels hint at brief ridging over the central and northern plains\non Tuesday, resulting in a dry and perhaps slightly warmer day,\nbut then longer range models show an upper low swinging from\nsouthwestern SD into the region.  We're still a ways out from \nthis, so models have been inconsistent as to how this low/trough \nwill affect the region, but will be monitoring as we move into \nnext week. There is potential for widespread precipitation the\nlatter half of the week depending on the track of this system.",
